{
  "$comment": "RUNNER SCHEDULING (#EXP-1613, #EXP-1617; src/runner.ts, ecode-core runner.rs). One step = one op at time `at` (unix ms). `enqueue{jobs: [{id, check, runsOn, needs: [job id], timeoutMin, expectMs?, skip?}]}` queues jobs (a planned `skip` completes at once with contract skip.conclusions); `beat{runner, labels, slots}` records a runner; `claim{runner}` → among the queued jobs whose needs all completed with a conclusion in checkConclusion.satisfies and whose `runsOn` labels the runner all has, while the runner has a free slot, the one expected to finish first (#EXP-1862: a flagged job first, then the smallest expectMs, else timeoutMin in ms; ties in queue order), so a 5-second job never waits behind a 30-minute one (result {job: id | null}); the server fills expectMs with the check's last run duration on the repository; `finish{runner, job, conclusion}` (only the runner running it); `cancel{jobs?}` (in-progress ones get a `kill`); `tick` = the clock: an in-progress job past its timeoutMin → kill + timed_out; a runner unseen for runner.runnerLostMs → its jobs fail (`runner_lost`); a job queued runner.queuedFlagMinutes → `queued_too_long`, once; a ready job no runner claimed within runner.queuedLimitMinutes of becoming ready (its queuedAt or its last need's completion) → timed_out (`unclaimed`), so its check row stops pending. After every op, a queued job whose need completed with any other conclusion, or was itself skipped for `needs`, is skipped (`needs`), to a fixpoint: a failure skips the whole chain below it. Refusals: unknown runner or job (not_found), finishing a job the runner does not run (not_running), a duplicate id (exists). `expect` = every job's status, conclusion, runner and flag, and the run's rollup (failure wins, then pending, then success).",
